#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1260.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167634} :activity/id #uuid "5d719a4c-ab32-5d8b-b2a7-90e293dc6083" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050770 :topic/id #uuid "6550b696-b7fd-5957-83f4-7ee02f7386ff" :topic/math-academy-id 3278} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167634 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168070 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $A$ is a $m\\times n$ matrix, and \n\n$$\nA=U \\Sigma V^T\n$$\n\nis a singular value decomposition of $A.$ If the $i$th singular value of $A$ is nonzero, then the $i$th column of the orthogonal matrix $U$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $\\mathbf u_i$ and $\\mathbf v_i$ are the $i$th columns of the orthogonal matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively.  \n\nThe formula above works whenever $\\sigma_i\\neq 0.$ But how do we find the $i$th column of $U$ in cases where $\\sigma_i = 0?$ It turns out that we can find the corresponding left singular vector using the geometric properties of singular values.\n\nFor example, suppose that\n\n![](images/fa/fab8a223fc22100592865eeb8b147313f4a635ffa3791863826ca6330730cc58.svg)\n\nand $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ where\n![](images/58/58af793e95fd96171530b2b63e74d12e965fb02eb81af42c8fdb3c9289a04b11.svg)\nThe matrices $\\Sigma$ and $V$ can be found using the methods we've seen before. So, our task now is to find the matrix $U.$ \n\nFrom $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that the singular values of $A$ are $\\sigma_1=4$ and $\\sigma_2=0.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 3 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince the second singular value is $\\sigma_2=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ However, we know that $U$ is an orthogonal matrix. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n![](images/78/781532700996b7a60101f204b934483da264ecab6acd6bfeda97141669308b6c.svg)\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u}_2 = 0 \\qquad \\textrm{and} \\qquad \\|\\mathbf{u}_2\\|=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's consider each condition in turn:\n\n- The equation $\\mathbf{u}_1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u}_2 = 0$  is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{{2}} x_1 +  \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} x_2 = 0.\n$$\n\nSeeking a nonzero solution to this equation, we get the vector \n![](images/75/7521799052487c5668d209c97ad6779f4258ecc5710ac81f2135966bf39974d3.svg)\n\n- Normalizing the vector we just found, we get\n![](images/30/30cc0b825690ed4dcd0bd672563f37bc6e621be092db70c4b46dafbf22db10c4.svg)\n\nTherefore, our orthogonal matrix $U$ is given by\n\n![](images/92/92ca53d48aed0c8435f89db4e11c022bc27ecb7a13d741e03270e0c981f639ef.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "01e70dc7-d85e-5e8c-939b-e7cd2c8b9ec3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5024 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5235213b-71c0-5372-9b7e-3f7b5abf5c6b" :step/math-academy-id 31418 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167636}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167635 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166799 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166637 :question/id #uuid "c69f45e6-f7e8-5339-9de9-0168b5a11e31" :question/math-academy-id "e-8437"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "30b55397-ef25-5e49-bee0-eb619e97b6ad" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167425} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404898} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404905}] :knowledge-point/title "The Singular Value Decomposition of a 2x2 Matrix With Repeated Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "b8cbe873-c1e3-5e8a-a689-8e8116d5b70e" :step/math-academy-id 31419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167636 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166800 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166638 :question/id #uuid "0324936b-a3b5-50ae-b064-dfff93c4dafb" :question/math-academy-id "e-8438"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "99270a04-7542-5545-92a3-cf13464ef8bc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050726}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167397} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371297} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420427}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Columns of the First Matrix Given Part of an SVD With a Zero Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "86c7507c-0f40-558d-b0f8-9cb4d481e270" :step/math-academy-id 31420 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167637}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167637 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166801 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166639 :question/id #uuid "4775354e-9a25-5e24-a1ef-57265f724a8b" :question/math-academy-id "e-8439"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f0dc37d-a22e-572c-933d-f3490ad356de" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420426}] :knowledge-point/title "The Singular Value Decomposition of a 2x2 Matrix With a Zero Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "af4cc601-95a5-50bc-8b95-471cf44bc75f" :step/math-academy-id 31421 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167657}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168073 :tutorial/content "It follows from the definitions that a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ has two repeated singular values $\\sigma_1= \\sigma_2= \\sigma$ if and only if the matrix  $A^TA$ has two repeated eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2 = \\sigma^2.$  Thus, since $A^TA$ is diagonalizable (because it is symmetric), the eigenspace corresponding to $\\sigma^2$ must be two-dimensional. Therefore, \n\n![](images/df/df266614e2576526353248439b7a17431d5e9e43153e1ba225adab3a89321373.svg)\n\nConsequently, we can use any pair of unit orthogonal vectors as a basis for the eigenspace corresponding to $\\sigma^2.$ \n\nLet's illustrate this with an example. Consider the following matrix:\n\n![](images/ee/ee74c3d4ed7e32388d06b47ed79789d768b54dee61e64d6db57c3eeb582089ae.svg)\n\nLet's find a singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute $A^T \\! A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}13 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: Notice that since $A^T \\! A$ is diagonal, the singular values of $A$ are \n\n$$\n\\sigma_1 = \\sigma_2 = \\sqrt{13}.\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of $A^T \\! A$): Since the singular values are equal, we can always take as solutions of $(A^T\\!A-13I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}$ the unit eigenvectors \n![](images/d1/d1770843817b2557b032743e186dd27106374fef90a2a8e8bb3cfe77b2eb0398.svg)\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute $\\mathbf u_1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute $\\mathbf u_2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U,$ $\\Sigma,$ and $V\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/c9/c950cf3aff8f9ab47ede762fa9c4451c5e7fc1e78e12fca064ee87d89fcb571e.svg)\n\n**Note:** By swapping the order of eigenvectors in step 3, we swap the vectors $\\mathbf{u}_1$ and $\\mathbf{u}_2$ in step 4. However, the resulting matrices (with swapped columns) will give us a valid singular decomposition too." :tutorial/id #uuid "9a6bbc33-1584-5b2f-aab5-628e9c95743a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5040 :tutorial/title "Singular Value Decomposition of 2x2 Matrices With Repeated Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "967ac37b-6901-5b91-988c-19fc53224cc6" :step/math-academy-id 31529 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167635}}] :activity/title "Singular Value Decomposition of 2x2 Matrices With Zero or Repeated Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167041}]}
